rota lap 4281, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels. Its threshold page states the test outright: a claim of a boundary is tested by pressing on it, and if the hand passes through, the boundary was a wish. Its cardinal seat is TAME_CORE.md, read as "a reflex that has drifted from its witness is a fence post rotted at the base, firm to the eye and loose to the hand." Pressing TAME Core's eleven Rye reflexes one at a time is what surfaced this lap's whole finding.

think Opened cold and the runner refused: a stale run lock from a dead lap, pid 3285504, group leader gone, no child, zero progress for 25 minutes. Resolved the pid through /proc/<pid>/cwd to this tree before signalling anything, then used the repair the runner itself prints -- kill -TERM, which runs its own EXIT trap and releases the lock. Never a pattern match; the baton's fifth and sixth firings are exactly that.
think Cold pass: 189 guards, 1,544s, 184 green, 2 red, 3 gated. Reds first.
think reds_citation named its own repair on one line: construction/archive/REDS-fold-recital.md:520 claim=%614 path=rows-613. git log -L on that line attributed it to commit 3539e3861 -- my own lap of this morning. My renumbering sweep, carrying my rows from %613 to %614/%615 after a rebase, was aimed at the FILE and took the neighbouring line with it, rewriting a peer's published %613. Repaired to %613, which is what the shelf it links and that shelf's own row header both say.
think standing_equipment red is downstream: it reads the run ledger, which held dated_path, season_leaf_choir and reds_citation as red. Re-ran the two that were stale through the runner by name -- reds_citation green, season_leaf_choir green at 33 of 33, the repair I landed this morning finally recorded.
think dated_path stays red and stays Keaton's. refs_lost=100 over a ceiling of 85, and the repairable class lost_promised_living reads 0 -- every one of the hundred is testimony nobody may repair or a mention that promises nothing. The scan's own header declines to move its gate, on the ground that a gate a lap moves for itself is a ceiling raised by a side door. I left it red rather than raising anything.
think Then the rota's own work. TAME_CORE.md names a checkable surface of four scripts and, twenty lines above it, eleven Rye reflexes. I pressed each reflex against the guards: std.debug.assert(, Self = @This(), usingnamespace, FIXME, dbg(, copyForwards, copyBackwards, the compound assert, the memcpy sites, camelCase, parseInt(, Ed25519 -- every one answered with a named ban or ratchet. The chapter allocator answered with nothing. git grep -il arenaallocator -- tools/ returns zero.
think Measured before deciding the shape: zero authored .rye files name ArenaAllocator and zero name GardenAllocator, across 1,958 tracked sources. A population at zero is the cheapest moment to seat a gate, because the ceiling and the wall are one number and no lap inherits a backlog -- so this is a wall rather than a ratchet, and the next arrival reds on the lap it enters.
think Two counters rather than one, because the two fail differently and want different cures: reaching past the season allocator for the inherited type, and renaming it into the word inherited-names.md reserves for Tally's own region. The canonical rename spells both words on one line, so the readings are exclusive and the line is charged to the stolen name -- asserted in the control, because a doubled count makes one defect look like two and a repair look half-done.

obs The finding is not that anyone broke the rule -- nobody has. It is that nothing could have told us if they had, and the reason it stayed invisible is that every reflex beside it on the same list was held. A page carrying a list of guards and a list of reflexes reads as though the first covers the second, and those two lists on TAME_CORE.md had been drifting apart in plain sight.
obs The two rows of this lap are the same fault in opposite directions, which is why they fold to one shelf. One boundary was absent -- three law pages describing a fence with no post anywhere. One was too wide -- a sweep aimed at a file rather than at the rows it owned. Both were repaired by putting the boundary into a mechanism rather than into a habit.
obs Against myself, twice, and the rebase answered both. My renumber was real and a peer had already booked it as %621 an hour earlier, with the one-character repair landed twice from two other hands -- so that row and that edit both withdrew whole, and my surviving row renumbered three times -- %622, %623, %626 -- as a peer spent each in turn. Every citation spelled the stamp, so each move cost one line. Against myself, twice. The renumber was mine, and the derived-spine law had written that exact sentence down -- one such sweep ran as a blind sed that rewrote a peer's row -- on a page I read this same morning. Knowing a hazard by name does not scope a command; the scope has to be in the command. And the card block I first wrote for this lap ran 2,300 bytes against a 666-byte elder, on a pin with 54 bytes of slack: the study belongs on the shelf and the card holds a pointer.
obs What is not proven, said plainly: whether a file reaching no arena should reach one -- TAME_GUIDANCE names a freestanding case with no Init at all -- and whether an arena reached correctly is then used within its bound, which is the width and assert surface other guards already read.

